#lang: en

#comment: PicoDoc Language Reference
#comment: Authoritative documentation of every language feature.

#title: PicoDoc Language Reference

#h2: Overview

PicoDoc is a macro-based markup language that compiles to HTML. Every construct
in the language (headings, bold text, links, user-defined templates) is a macro
call. This regularity means there is only one syntax to learn.

Design principles\:

[#ul :
  [#* : [#b : One abstraction] \: macros are the only construct.]
  [#* : [#b : Named arguments] \: all arguments are named, eliminating
    positional ambiguity.]
  [#* : [#b : Strict parsing] \: invalid input is rejected with clear error
    messages rather than silently guessed at.]
  [#* : [#b : UTF-8 only] \: no encoding detection or fallback.]
  [#* : [#b : HTML target] \: the primary output format is HTML.]
]

The file extension is [#code : .pdoc].

#h2: Lexical Structure

#h3: Identifiers

An identifier consists of one or more letters, digits, dots, or special
characters\:

[#code : """
    ! $ % & * + - / @ ^ _ ~
    """]

Dots allow namespacing (e.g. [#code : env.mode]). The identifier terminates at
the first character that is not in the set above, such as whitespace, colons,
quotes, brackets, equals signs, hash, or backslash.

#h3: Escape Sequences

Backslash is the escape prefix. Backslash followed by any character not on the
valid list for the current context is a syntax error.

#p: [#b : Prose escapes] (in body text and barewords)\:

#table:
  Sequence | Produces
  [#code"\\\\"] | Literal backslash
  [#code"\\#"] | Literal \#
  [#code"\\["] | Literal \[
  [#code"\\]"] | Literal \]
  [#code"\\:"] | Literal \:
  [#code"\\="] | Literal \=
  [#code"\\xHH"] | Unicode codepoint U+0000 to U+00FF
  [#code"\\UHHHHHHHH"] | Unicode codepoint (8 hex digits)

#p: [#b : String escapes] (inside interpreted string literals)\:

#table:
  Sequence | Produces
  [#code"\\\\"] | Literal backslash
  [#code"\\\""] | Literal double quote
  [#code"\\["] | Enter code mode (macro expansion)
  [#code"\\n"] | Newline
  [#code"\\t"] | Tab
  [#code"\\xHH"] | Unicode codepoint U+0000 to U+00FF
  [#code"\\UHHHHHHHH"] | Unicode codepoint (8 hex digits)

Note that [#code"\\["] has different meaning in the two contexts\: in prose it
produces a literal \[, while in strings it enters code mode.

#h3: Comments

The \#comment macro removes its body from the output entirely.

#code: """
    #comment: This text will not appear in the output.

    [#comment : Multi-line comments
      use the bracketed form.]
    """

#h2: Macro Calls

There are two forms of macro call\: unbracketed and bracketed. The parser does
not need to consult macro definitions to parse either form.

#h3: Unbracketed Form

A macro call begins with \# followed immediately by the macro identifier.
After the identifier, the parser examines the next token\:

[#ol :
  [#* : If the token matches [#code"identifier="] (no whitespace before
    the equals sign), it enters argument mode and consumes name\=value pairs.]
  [#* : If the token is a colon, it enters body mode.]
  [#* : If the token is a string literal opening, it enters body mode
    with the string as body.]
  [#* : Otherwise the call is complete with no arguments and no body.
    Remaining text on the line is prose.]
]

#code: """
    #title: Document Title
    #h2: Section Heading
    #hr
    #p"Explicit paragraph."
    """

In argument mode, the parser consumes name\=value pairs. If the next token is
not a name\=value pair, a colon, or a string literal, the call is complete and
the remaining text on the line is prose. After a colon body (to end of line) or
a string literal body, the call is also complete and any remaining text is
prose.

#code: """
    #code language=python : print("hello")
    #meta name=viewport content="width=device-width"
    """

#h3: Bracketed Form

A macro call enclosed in square brackets\: \[#identifier ...\]. Named
arguments may appear as name\=value pairs separated by whitespace. A colon or
string literal introduces body content. Everything until the matching closing
bracket is body, which may span multiple lines and paragraphs.

#code: """
    [#b : bold text]
    [#url link="https://example.com" text="Click here"]
    [#code language=python : """
        def hello():
            print("world")
        """]
    """

Bare text inside brackets that is not a name\=value pair and not preceded by
a colon or string literal is a syntax error.

#h3: Named Arguments

Named arguments use the syntax [#code"name=value"] with no whitespace before
the equals sign. Whitespace after the equals sign is permitted.

Argument values can be\:

[#ul :
  [#* : [#b : Bareword] (a simple word with no syntactic characters)\:
    [#code"language=python"]]
  [#* : [#b : String literal] (interpreted or raw)\:
    [#code"text=\"Click here\""]]
  [#* : [#b : Macro reference] (a zero-argument macro)\:
    [#code"link=#site-url"]]
  [#* : [#b : Bracketed call] (a macro call in brackets)\:
    [#code"style=[#get-style]"]]
]

#h3: The Body Argument

Body content is introduced by a colon or by a string literal\:

#p: [#b : Colon, inline body]\: text follows the colon on the same line.

#code: """
    #title: Document Title
    """

#p: [#b : Colon, paragraph body]\: nothing follows the colon on the line. The
next paragraph (contiguous non-blank lines) becomes the body.

#code: """
    #p:
    This paragraph spans
    multiple lines.
    """

#p: [#b : String literal body]\: a string literal serves as body. A colon
before the string is optional.

#code: """
    #p"A string literal paragraph."
    #**"bold text"
    [#code language=python """
        def hello():
            pass
        """]
    """

#p: [#b : Bracketed body]\: in bracketed calls, body extends to the closing
bracket and may span multiple lines and paragraphs.

#code: """
    [#ul :
      #*: First item
      #*: Second item
      #*: Third item
    ]
    """

If no colon and no string literal is present, the macro call has no body.

#h2: String Literals

#h3: Interpreted Strings

Delimited by a single double quote on each end. Escape sequences are processed
but macro calls are NOT automatically scanned for.

#code: """
    "A simple string."
    "Contains a tab:\there."
    "Contains a newline:\nSecond line."
    "A literal quote: \" inside."
    """

To embed macro calls within an interpreted string, use [#code"\\["] to enter
code mode. Code mode ends at the matching closing \]. Normal bracketed macro
call syntax applies inside [#code"\\[...\\]"].

#code: """
    "Hello, \[#name]!"
    "Dear \[#target], welcome to \[#place]."
    """

#h3: Raw Strings

Delimited by three or more double quote characters. The closing delimiter must
have exactly the same number of quotes as the opening. Contents are completely
opaque (no escapes, no macro processing).

#code: """""
    """This is raw: \n is literal and #name is not expanded."""
    """"Contains """ three quotes inside.""""
    """""

#h3: Empty String

The empty string is always [#code"\"\""] (two double quotes). An empty raw
string is not possible because the opening quotes run into the closing.

#h3: Whitespace Stripping

Both interpreted and raw strings apply the same whitespace rules\:

[#ol :
  [#* : If the remainder of the opening delimiter's line is blank (whitespace
    only), that remainder is discarded.]
  [#* : If the beginning of the closing delimiter's line is blank (whitespace
    only), that whitespace is discarded.]
  [#* : If the closing delimiter's leading whitespace appears identically on
    ALL other lines of the string, that common prefix is stripped from every
    line (indentation stripping).]
]

This allows indenting string content to match the surrounding markup without
affecting the result\:

#code: """
    [#code language=python : """
        def hello():
            print("world")
        """]
    """

The four spaces before [#code"def"] and [#code"print"] are stripped because
they match the indentation of the closing delimiter.

#h3: Adjacent String Restriction

After a string literal closes, the next character must not be a double quote.
At least one non-quote character must separate consecutive string literals.
This prevents a mismatched quote from silently cascading through the file.

#h2: Builtin Macros

PicoDoc has 26 builtin macros in 7 categories, plus 7 aliases.

#h3: Structural Macros

#h4: \#title

Aliases\: \#h1, \#-. Top-level document title. Renders as [#code"<h1>"].
Takes body, no parameters.

#code: """
    #title: My Document
    #-: Alias form
    """

#h4: \#h2

Alias\: \#--. Section heading. Renders as [#code"<h2>"]. Takes body, no
parameters.

#h4: \#h3

Alias\: \#---. Subsection heading. Renders as [#code"<h3>"]. Takes body, no
parameters.

#h4: \#h4, \#h5, \#h6

Lower-level headings. Render as [#code"<h4>"] through [#code"<h6>"]. Take
body, no parameters. No aliases.

#h4: \#p

Paragraph. Renders as [#code"<p>"]. Takes body, no parameters. Bare text
paragraphs (not inside a macro call) are implicitly wrapped in \#p.

#code: """
    #p: An explicit paragraph.

    This bare text is also a paragraph.
    """

#h4: \#hr

Horizontal rule. Renders as [#code"<hr>"]. No parameters, no body.

#h3: Inline Macros

#h4: \#b

Alias\: \#**. Bold text. Renders as [#code"<strong>"]. Takes body, no
parameters.

#code: """
    This is #**"bold" text.
    This is [#b : also bold] text.
    """

#h4: \#i

Alias\: \#__. Italic text. Renders as [#code"<em>"]. Takes body, no
parameters.

#code: """
    This is #__"italic" text.
    This is [#i : also italic] text.
    """

#h4: \#url

Hyperlink. Renders as [#code"<a>"]. Parameters\:

#table:
  Parameter | Required | Description
  link | Yes | URL target (href)
  text | No | Link text (defaults to empty)

Takes optional body. If body is provided, it is used as the link content
(overriding the text parameter).

#code: """
    [#url link="https://example.com" text="Click here"]
    [#url link="https://example.com" : Click here]
    """

#h3: Code and Literal Macros

#h4: \#code

Code / monospace text. Renders as [#code"<code>"] (inline) or as
a [#code"<pre><code>"] block. Parameters\:

#table:
  Parameter | Required | Description
  language | No | Programming language for syntax class

Takes body.

#code: """
    Inline: [#code : print()]
    Block with language:
    [#code language=python : """
        def hello():
            print("world")
        """]
    """

#h4: \#literal

Prevents re-expansion of its body. Any macro calls or escapes within the body
are passed through as literal text. Useful for documenting PicoDoc syntax or
for output from external filters. Takes body, no parameters.

#code: """"
    #literal"""
    The #b macro makes text bold.
    Use [#url link="..." text="..."] for links.
    """
    """"

#h3: List Macros

#h4: \#ul

Unordered list. Renders as [#code"<ul>"]. Takes body, no parameters. Body must
contain only \#* (list item) elements.

#h4: \#ol

Ordered list. Renders as [#code"<ol>"]. Takes body, no parameters. Body must
contain only \#* (list item) elements.

#h4: \#*

Alias\: \#li. List item. Renders as [#code"<li>"]. Takes body, no parameters.
Must appear inside \#ul or \#ol.

#code: """
    [#ul :
      #*: First item
      #*: Second with #**"bold"
      [#* : Third with a sublist
        [#ul :
          #*: Nested A
          #*: Nested B
        ]
      ]
    ]

    [#ol :
      #*: Step one
      #*: Step two
    ]
    """

#h3: Table Macros

#h4: \#table

Table container. Renders as [#code"<table>"]. Takes body, no parameters.
Supports two forms\:

#p: [#b : Pipe-delimited form]\: the body is parsed as pipe-separated rows.
The first row becomes headers (\#th), subsequent rows become data (\#td).

#code: """
    #table:
      Name | Age | Status
      Alice | 30 | Active
      Bob | 25 | Inactive
    """

#p: [#b : Explicit form]\: the body contains \#tr, \#th, and \#td calls
directly.

#code: """
    [#table :
      [#tr : [#th: Name] [#th: Age]]
      [#tr : [#td: Alice] [#td: 30]]
    ]
    """

#h4: \#tr

Table row. Renders as [#code"<tr>"]. Takes body, no parameters.

#h4: \#td

Table data cell. Renders as [#code"<td>"]. Parameters\:

#table:
  Parameter | Required | Description
  span | No | Column span (colspan attribute)

Takes body.

#h4: \#th

Table header cell. Renders as [#code"<th>"]. Same parameters as \#td. Takes
body.

#code: """
    [#table :
      [#tr : [#th: Name] [#th: Age]]
      [#tr : [#td: Alice] [#td: 30]]
      [#tr : [#td span=2 : Total: 1 person]]
    ]
    """

#h3: Document Macros

#h4: \#lang

Sets the lang attribute on the HTML [#code"<html>"] element. Takes body (the
language code), no parameters.

#code: """
    #lang: en
    """

#h4: \#meta

Adds a [#code"<meta>"] tag to the document head. Parameters\:

#table:
  Parameter | Required | Description
  name | No | Meta name attribute
  property | No | Meta property attribute (e.g. Open Graph)
  content | Yes | Meta content value

No body.

#code: """
    #meta name=viewport content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1"
    #meta property="og:title" content="PicoDoc Reference"
    """

#h4: \#link

Adds a [#code"<link>"] tag to the document head. Parameters\:

#table:
  Parameter | Required | Description
  rel | Yes | Relationship (e.g. stylesheet)
  href | Yes | URL of the linked resource

No body.

#code: """
    #link rel=stylesheet href="style.css"
    """

#h4: \#script

Adds a [#code"<script>"] element. Parameters\:

#table:
  Parameter | Required | Description
  src | No | URL of external script

Takes optional body (inline script content). Use either the src parameter or
body, not both.

#code: """
    #script src="app.js"
    [#script : """
        console.log("Hello from PicoDoc");
        """]
    """

#h3: Expansion-Time Macros

These macros are fully resolved during evaluation and do not appear in the
final AST.

The \#comment macro removes its body from the output. Takes body, no
parameters.

The \#set macro defines a user macro. See the User-Defined Macros section
below.

#h4: \#ifeq

String equality test. Returns body if lhs equals rhs, empty string
otherwise. Parameters\:

#table:
  Parameter | Required | Description
  lhs | Yes | Left-hand side value
  rhs | Yes | Right-hand side value

Takes body.

#h4: \#ifne

String inequality test. Returns body if lhs does not equal rhs, empty
string otherwise. Same parameters as \#ifeq. Takes body.

#h4: \#ifset

Tests whether a macro is defined. Returns body if the named macro exists,
empty string otherwise. Parameters\:

#table:
  Parameter | Required | Description
  name | Yes | Macro name to test

Takes body.

#h4: \#include

Inserts the contents of another file. The included file is parsed and expanded
as if its contents appeared at the call site. Parameters\:

#table:
  Parameter | Required | Description
  file | Yes | Path to the file to include

No body.

#h3: Alias Table

All 7 aliases mapped to their canonical names\:

#table:
  Alias | Canonical
  \#- | \#title
  \#h1 | \#title
  \#-- | \#h2
  \#--- | \#h3
  \#** | \#b
  \#__ | \#i
  \#li | \#*

#h2: User-Defined Macros

#h3: Defining Macros with \#set

The \#set macro defines a new macro. It must always appear in bracketed form
and at the top level (not inside macro bodies).

#code: """
    [#set name=version : 1.0]
    [#set name=greeting target=? body=? : Dear #target, #body Kind regards.]
    [#set name=box style=default body=? : [#p : (#style) #body]]
    """

The name argument is required and specifies the macro name. Additional
arguments become the macro's parameters.

#h3: Parameters

Parameters can be\:

[#ul :
  [#* : [#b : Required] (marked with ?, the caller must supply a value)\:
    [#code"target=?"]]
  [#* : [#b : With default] (given a default value used when the caller omits
    the argument)\: [#code"style=default"]]
]

If the macro uses a body parameter, it must be declared as [#code"body=?"]
(required) or [#code"body=default"] and must be the last parameter.

Default values are evaluated during the resolution pass after all definitions
are collected. A default value may be a macro call, which will be expanded
during resolution.

#h3: Calling User Macros

User macros are called exactly like builtin macros\:

#code: """
    #version
    [#greeting target=World : thank you for your support.]
    [#box style=fancy : Content here.]
    """

A macro with no arguments is essentially a variable\:

#code: """
    [#set name=version : 1.0]
    The version is #version.
    """

#h3: Out-of-Order Definitions

Macros may be used before they are defined. The compiler collects all \#set
definitions in a first pass before resolving and expanding\:

#code: """
    #p: The project is #project-name.
    [#set name=project-name : PicoDoc]
    """

#h3: Duplicate Detection

Duplicate macro definitions at the same scope are an immediate error.

#h3: Scope and Shadowing

Macro invocation creates a new scope at the call site. Local argument names
shadow any definitions outside the macro call with the same name.

#h3: Macro References as Argument Values

A zero-argument macro (variable) can be passed as an argument value using
the \#identifier form\:

#code: """
    [#set name=site-url : https://example.com]
    [#url link=#site-url text="Our site"]
    """

#h2: Conditionals

PicoDoc has no expression language. Conditions are limited to simple
comparisons via specialised macros.

The \#ifeq macro returns body if lhs equals rhs\:

#code: """
    [#ifeq lhs=#mode rhs=draft : This document is a draft.]
    """

The \#ifne macro returns body if lhs does not equal rhs\:

#code: """
    [#ifne lhs=#mode rhs=production : Not yet published.]
    """

The \#ifset macro returns body if the named macro is defined\:

#code: """
    [#ifset name=env.author : Written by [#env.author].]
    """

Conditionals can be nested\:

#code: """
    [#ifeq lhs=#mode rhs=draft :
      [#ifset name=env.author : Draft by [#env.author].]
    ]
    """

#h2: Includes

The \#include macro inserts the contents of another file at the call site. The
included file is parsed and expanded as if it appeared inline.

#code: """
    [#include file="header.pdoc"]

    #h2: Main Content

    #p: The body of the document.

    [#include file="footer.pdoc"]
    """

A common pattern is to use includes for shared headers and footers across
multiple documents.

#h2: Global Environment (env.*)

The env.* namespace provides global values accessible to all macros, including
external filters.

#h3: Defining Environment Values

Values can be set from three sources (listed in ascending precedence)\:

[#ol :
  [#* : [#b : Config file] \: under the \[env\] section in
    [#code"picodoc.toml"]]
  [#* : [#b : CLI arguments] \: using [#code"-e name=value"] (repeatable)]
  [#* : [#b : Document-level \#set] \: for example
    \[#set name\=env.mode \: draft\]]
]

Document-level definitions have the highest precedence. The document author
has final say.

#h3: Accessing Environment Values

Environment values are accessed as zero-argument macros\:

#code: """
    #env.mode
    [#env.author]
    """

They are inherited through nested macro calls. A macro body can reference
\#env.mode and it resolves from the global environment.

#h3: Immutability

Environment values are global and immutable once set. They cannot be
overridden locally within macro bodies. This prevents confusing
action-at-a-distance where a macro silently changes global state.

#h2: External Filters

External filters allow users to define macros as command-line programs in any
language.

#h3: Protocol

The filter receives a JSON object on stdin containing all named arguments
(including body if present) and all env.* values\:

#code: """
    {"arg1": "value1", "body": "the body text", "env": {"mode": "draft"}}
    """

The filter returns PicoDoc markup on stdout. The multi-pass evaluator expands
any macro calls in the output on subsequent passes.

A filter that wants to return final HTML (preventing re-expansion) wraps its
output in \#literal.

#h3: Discovery

The converter searches for filter executables in this order\:

[#ol :
  [#* : A filters/ directory alongside the document]
  [#* : Directories specified via [#code"--filter-path"] or config]
  [#* : The system $PATH]
]

The executable name maps to the macro name.

#h3: Timeout

A configurable timeout (default 5 seconds, set via [#code"--filter-timeout"]
or config) kills long-running filters. Non-zero exit codes are treated as
expansion errors with the filter's stderr included in the error message.

#h2: Configuration File

Place a [#code"picodoc.toml"] next to your input file (or point to one with
the [#code"--config"] flag). CLI flags override config values.

#code: """
    [env]
    title = "My Document"
    author = "Jane Doe"

    [css]
    files = ["style.css", "code.css"]

    [js]
    files = ["main.js"]

    [meta]
    viewport = "width=device-width, initial-scale=1"

    [filters]
    paths = ["./filters"]
    timeout = 10.0
    """

#h2: Command-Line Interface

Usage\:

#code: """
    uv run picodoc [OPTIONS] INPUT
    """

#h3: Options

#table:
  Flag | Description
  [#code"-o FILE"] | Write output to FILE instead of stdout
  [#code"-e NAME=VALUE"] | Set an environment variable (repeatable)
  [#code"--css FILE"] | Inject a CSS file into the head (repeatable)
  [#code"--js FILE"] | Inject a JS file into the head (repeatable)
  [#code"--meta NAME=VALUE"] | Add a meta tag (repeatable)
  [#code"--filter-path DIR"] | Extra filter search directory (repeatable)
  [#code"--filter-timeout SECS"] | Filter execution timeout (default 5.0)
  [#code"--config FILE"] | Config file path (auto-discovers picodoc.toml)
  [#code"--watch"] | Watch for changes and recompile on save
  [#code"--debug"] | Dump the AST to stderr

#h3: Exit Codes

#table:
  Code | Meaning
  0 | Success
  1 | Syntax error (lex or parse failure)
  2 | Evaluation error or invalid arguments

#h2: Expansion Model

PicoDoc processes a document through a pipeline of stages\:

[#ol :
  [#* : [#b : Lexing] \: source text is tokenized into a stream of tokens.]
  [#* : [#b : Parsing] \: tokens are assembled into an AST (abstract syntax
    tree). The parser is definition-independent; it does not need to know which
    macros are defined.]
  [#* : [#b : Collection] \: all \#set definitions are gathered from the AST
    without evaluating them.]
  [#* : [#b : Resolution] \: definitions are resolved and default values are
    evaluated, which may themselves require expansion passes.]
  [#* : [#b : Expansion] \: the evaluator walks the AST expanding macro nodes,
    marks fully-expanded nodes as complete, and re-walks until convergence.
    Expansion-time builtins (\#set, \#ifeq, \#include, \#comment, \#table pipe
    form) are resolved and removed. Only text nodes and render-time builtins
    remain.]
  [#* : [#b : Rendering] \: the renderer maps the expanded AST to HTML,
    validates nesting, handles HTML escaping, and wraps the output in a
    proper document structure (doctype, head, body).]
]

#h3: Multi-Pass Expansion

Expansion is convergence-based. The evaluator keeps walking the AST until no
unexpanded macro calls remain. This allows macros to produce output containing
other macro calls that are expanded on subsequent passes (e.g. \#table pipe
form emits \#tr/\#th/\#td calls).

#h3: Call Stack Depth

A global maximum call stack depth (configurable, default 64) catches infinite
or excessive recursion. Exceeding this limit is an evaluation error.

#h3: Preventing Re-Expansion

The \#literal macro prevents its body from being re-expanded. This is useful
for\:

[#ul :
  [#* : Documenting PicoDoc syntax within PicoDoc documents]
  [#* : External filters that return final HTML]
  [#* : Any situation where text containing \# or \[ should be treated as
    literal output]
]
